  "Number": 3,
  "Question": "If \\(x=1+2^p\\) and \\(y=1+2^{-p},\\) then \\(y\\) in terms of \\(x\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{x+1}{x-1}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{x+2}{x-1}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{x}{x-1}\\)",
    "D": "\\(2-x\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{x-1}{x}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The opposite exponents make \\(2^p\\) and \\(2^{-p}\\) reciprocals. Since \\(x=1+2^p\\), we have\n\\[\n2^p=x-1,\n\\qquad 2^{-p}=\\frac1{x-1}.\n\\]\nFor real \\(p\\), \\(2^p>0\\), so \\(x>1\\) and the denominator is nonzero. Substituting into the formula for \\(y\\) gives\n\\[\ny=1+\\frac1{x-1}\n=\\frac{x-1+1}{x-1}\n=\\frac{x}{x-1}.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\boxed{y=x/(x-1)}\\), choice C.",
